<h2>A clean question of law, two decades old</h2>
<p>The underlying facts were brief. On 18 July 1996, at about 8:15 in the morning, police raided the respondent&rsquo;s house in Sultan Puri and allegedly recovered one hundred pouches of illicit liquor, one hundred millilitres each, held without licence or permit. The trial that followed was unremarkable — four prosecution witnesses, a Section 313 CrPC statement denying the evidence, no defence evidence — until the judgment. The trial court acquitted on 23 May 2003 without deciding the merits at all: in its view, Section 75 of the Punjab Excise Act permitted cognizance only on the complaint or report of an &ldquo;Excise Officer,&rdquo; and the SHO&rsquo;s police report under Section 173 CrPC was not that.</p>

<h2>The missing notification</h2>
<p>Before the High Court, the State produced what the trial court had overlooked: a notification of the Lieutenant Governor dated 7 September 1966, issued under Section 3(8) read with Section 11 of the Act, investing police officers with excise powers. It relied on <em>Chattar Singh v. State</em> (1985) and <em>State v. Jaswant Singh</em> (2013), both holding that police officers so invested answer the statutory definition of Excise Officer. The respondent&rsquo;s counter was that it was never shown that the particular reporting officer was duly empowered.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>Trial court, 2003</h3><p>Only an Excise Officer may report under Section 75; an SHO&rsquo;s Section 173 CrPC report cannot substitute. Acquittal at the threshold, merits untouched.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>High Court, 2026</h3><p>Police officers invested with excise powers by the 1966 notification are Excise Officers; the SHO was competent to file the final report. The threshold bar never existed.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Following <em>Chattar Singh</em>, Justice Dudeja held that &ldquo;the determinative consideration is whether the police officer concerned had been duly invested with the powers,&rdquo; and on that footing found the SHO &ldquo;was competent to file the final report&rdquo; under Section 173 CrPC. The acquittal therefore rested on a legally erroneous premise. The Court set aside the judgment of 23 May 2003 and remanded the matter to the trial court to decide it afresh on merits after hearing both sides, expressly recording that &ldquo;no opinion is expressed on the merits of the prosecution case.&rdquo; The respondent was directed to appear before the CJM, North-West District, Rohini, on 20 November 2026.</p>

<h2>What the decision illustrates</h2>
<p>Three points of broader interest emerge. First, cognizance bars in special statutes — provisions requiring a complaint or report by a designated officer — are genuine jurisdictional filters, but their application depends on the full delegation picture: notifications investing powers can transform who counts as the designated officer, and both sides must place that material before the trial court. Second, an acquittal founded purely on a threshold error of law is vulnerable in a State appeal in a way that an acquittal on evidence is not; the usual double-presumption protecting acquittals has little to grip when the merits were never decided. Third, the case is a sobering illustration of criminal litigation timelines: a 1996 raid, a 2003 acquittal, a 2004 appeal, and a 2026 remand sending the matter back for a fresh decision on merits — a reminder that jurisdictional shortcuts at trial can cost decades rather than save them.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>For today\'s practice:</strong> the Punjab Excise Act 1914 framework discussed here continues to govern old Delhi excise prosecutions, while the Delhi Excise Act, 2009 governs later offences. The principle, however, travels: wherever a statute restricts who may set the criminal law in motion, counsel should demand — and courts should examine — the actual instrument of empowerment before treating the restriction as breached.</p>
</div>
